WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
We want to describe a row whose positions form a cycle.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.600
If an entry moves past the last position, it should return to

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:06.960
the first.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.680
Ordinary polynomials do not do that automatically, so let us change the

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:12.960
rules to match the circle.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.540
Take the cyclic group generated by X, with the rule X to

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:18.380
the n equals one.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.940
Its elements are one, X, X squared, through X to the n

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:24.580
minus one.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.280
X is a formal marker for one step around the cycle, rather

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.100
than a number we are substituting.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.080
The integer group ring attaches an integer coefficient to each position and

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:36.760
adds the terms.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.380
We add coefficients as usual, and multiply with the rule X to

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.340
the n equals one.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.160
This is the quotient of integer polynomials by X to the n

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:46.820
minus one.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.220
In the quotient, that defining polynomial is zero.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.800
For n equal to four, X to the fourth is one, X

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.460
to the fifth is X, and X inverse is X cubed.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.060
For example, three X to the fifth becomes three X: the exponent

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.740
wraps once around the four positions.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.940
A negative exponent moves backward around the same cycle.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.180
Every element has a representative of degree less than n.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.180
Replace X to the n by one, X to the n plus

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.100
one by X, and keep reducing exponents.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.660
Collect the terms whose exponents land in the same position.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.260
The reduced representative is also unique.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.320
If two polynomials of degree less than n represent the same element,

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.020
their difference is a multiple of X to the n minus one.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.300
But any nonzero such multiple has degree at least n.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.280
The difference must therefore be zero.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.940
Thus the ordered coefficient list is exactly the same information as one

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.560
group ring element with degree less than n.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.960
Its positions are unambiguous.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.780
Next, we will see why multiplying this polynomial by X is precisely

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.720
a right cyclic shift of the row.
